    %%field Campaign.end_date help: The day it stops. Leave empty for an open-ended programme.
    %%field Campaign.budgeted_cost help: What you agreed to spend.
    %%field Campaign.actual_cost help: What it actually cost, which is the half that decides whether to run it again.
    %%field Campaign.expected_revenue help: The pipeline this campaign is expected to generate.
    %%field Campaign.expected_response_count help: How many responses you planned for, to compare against what arrived.
    %%field Campaign.target_audience help: Who this campaign was aimed at, in a sentence.
    %%field Campaign.owner_id help: The user accountable for this programme.

    %%field CampaignMember.campaign_id help: The campaign this person was targeted by.
    %%field CampaignMember.lead_id help: The lead who was targeted, when the target was not yet a known contact.
    %%field CampaignMember.contact_id help: The contact who was targeted, when they were already known to you.
    %%field CampaignMember.member_status help: Where this person got to in the campaign — sent, opened, responded.
    %%field CampaignMember.responded_at help: When they responded. Empty until they do.
    %%field CampaignMember.has_responded help: Set once any response arrives, so response rates can be counted without reading dates.

    %%field Opportunity.account_id help: The company this deal is with.
    %%field Opportunity.contact_id help: The person championing this deal inside the account.
    %%field Opportunity.campaign_id help: The campaign this deal can be attributed to, where there is one.
    %%field Opportunity.name help: A short description of what is being bought, as it reads in the pipeline list.
    %%field Opportunity.description help: The detail — what they need, what you proposed, what is still open.
    %%field Opportunity.amount help: The value of this deal. It should agree with its line items.
    %%field Opportunity.currency_code help: The currency the amount is in.
    %%field Opportunity.stage help: How far along this deal is. The stage is what the forecast is built from.
    %%field Opportunity.probability help: The 0-100 chance of winning. The forecasting rule derives it from the stage.
    %%field Opportunity.forecast_category help: Which forecast bucket this deal falls in — pipeline, best case, commit.
    %%field Opportunity.expected_close_date help: When you expect to win it. Moving this date is the single most watched change on a deal.
    %%field Opportunity.actual_close_date help: When it actually closed, won or lost. Set when the stage becomes final.
    %%field Opportunity.lead_source help: Where the deal originally came from.
    %%field Opportunity.next_step help: The one thing that has to happen next, and by when.
    %%field Opportunity.loss_reason help: Why the deal was lost. Required on a closed-lost deal, and the reason loss reports mean anything.
    %%field Opportunity.owner_id help: The user selling this deal.

    %%field OpportunityLineItem.opportunity_id help: The deal this line belongs to.
    %%field OpportunityLineItem.product_id help: What is being sold on this line.
    %%field OpportunityLineItem.quantity help: How many.
    %%field OpportunityLineItem.unit_price help: The price for one, before any discount on this line.
    %%field OpportunityLineItem.discount_percent help: The discount applied to this line, as a percentage.
    %%field OpportunityLineItem.line_total help: Quantity times unit price, less the discount. Calculated — do not type into it.
    %%field OpportunityLineItem.line_description help: Anything about this line the product name does not say.

    %%field Product.product_code help: Your own catalogue code for this product. Unique, and what an order refers to.
    %%field Product.name help: The product's name as it appears on a quote.
    %%field Product.description help: What it is and what it includes, in the words a customer would read.
    %%field Product.family help: The group this product belongs to, for reporting and for the product picker.
    %%field Product.list_price help: The published price before any discount.
    %%field Product.unit_cost help: What it costs you, which is what makes margin calculable.
    %%field Product.billing_frequency help: How often this is charged — once, monthly, annually.
    %%field Product.is_active help: Clear this to stop the product being added to new quotes without disturbing the ones it is already on.

    %%field Quote.quote_number help: The reference the customer will quote back at you. Unique.
    %%field Quote.opportunity_id help: The deal this quote prices.
    %%field Quote.account_id help: The company the quote is addressed to.
    %%field Quote.name help: A short title for this quote, so several versions can be told apart.
    %%field Quote.status help: Where this quote is — draft, waiting for approval, sent, accepted, rejected.
    %%field Quote.version_number help: Which revision this is. A new version is raised rather than an old one edited.
    %%field Quote.valid_until help: The day this offer expires.
    %%field Quote.subtotal help: The line items added up, before discount and tax.
    %%field Quote.discount_percent help: A discount across the whole quote. Past the approval threshold it needs a manager.
    %%field Quote.discount_amount help: The discount in money, where it was agreed as an amount rather than a percentage.
    %%field Quote.tax_amount help: Tax on the discounted subtotal.
    %%field Quote.grand_total help: What the customer will actually pay. Calculated — do not type into it.
    %%field Quote.approved_by_id help: The manager who approved this quote's discount. Empty until one does.
    %%field Quote.approved_at help: When approval was given.
    %%field Quote.approval_notes help: What the approver said — the conditions, or why they refused.
    %%field Quote.owner_id help: The user who raised this quote.

    %%field QuoteLineItem.quote_id help: The quote this line appears on.
    %%field QuoteLineItem.product_id help: What is being quoted on this line.
    %%field QuoteLineItem.quantity help: How many.
    %%field QuoteLineItem.unit_price help: The price for one, before any discount on this line.
    %%field QuoteLineItem.discount_percent help: The discount applied to this line, as a percentage.
    %%field QuoteLineItem.line_total help: Quantity times unit price, less the discount. Calculated — do not type into it.
    %%field QuoteLineItem.sort_order help: Where this line sits on the printed quote.

    %%field Contract.contract_number help: The reference this agreement is known by. Unique.
    %%field Contract.account_id help: The company that signed.
    %%field Contract.quote_id help: The accepted quote this contract was written from.
    %%field Contract.status help: Where the agreement is — drafted, out for signature, active, expired.
    %%field Contract.start_date help: The day the term begins.
    %%field Contract.end_date help: The day the term ends, and so the day the renewal conversation is already late.
    %%field Contract.term_months help: How long the term runs, in months.
    %%field Contract.annual_value help: What this agreement is worth per year. The renewal book is the sum of these.
    %%field Contract.auto_renew help: Set when the term rolls over unless someone cancels it.
    %%field Contract.renewal_notice_days help: How many days' notice either side must give. It decides when the renewal work has to start.
    %%field Contract.signed_by_id help: The user who countersigned on your side.
    %%field Contract.signed_at help: When it was signed.
    %%field Contract.owner_id help: The user accountable for this agreement and its renewal.

    %%field SupportCase.case_number help: The reference the customer quotes when they chase. Unique.
    %%field SupportCase.account_id help: The company reporting the problem. It decides which SLA applies.
    %%field SupportCase.contact_id help: The person who reported it, and who you will reply to.
    %%field SupportCase.sla_policy_id help: The response and resolution times this case is measured against.
    %%field SupportCase.subject help: The problem in one line, as it reads in the queue.
    %%field SupportCase.description help: What happened, what they expected, and what they have already tried.
    %%field SupportCase.case_type help: What kind of case this is — a question, an incident, a defect, a request.
    %%field SupportCase.priority help: How urgent it is. The triage rule sets it from type and account tier.
    %%field SupportCase.origin help: How the case reached you — email, phone, the web, the partner portal.
    %%field SupportCase.status help: Where the case is, from new through to closed.
    %%field SupportCase.first_response_due_at help: When the customer must have heard from a person. Calculated from the SLA on arrival.
    %%field SupportCase.first_response_at help: When they actually did. Empty until someone replies.
    %%field SupportCase.resolution_due_at help: When the case must be resolved by, under the SLA.
    %%field SupportCase.resolved_at help: When it actually was resolved.
    %%field SupportCase.is_sla_breached help: Set when a due time passed unmet. It is what breach reporting counts.
    %%field SupportCase.resolution_notes help: What fixed it, in enough detail that the next person with this problem is helped.
    %%field SupportCase.satisfaction_score help: What the customer scored the handling, 1 to 5, after it closed.
    %%field SupportCase.escalated_by_id help: The user who escalated this case, when one did.
    %%field SupportCase.owner_id help: The agent handling it.

    %%field SlaPolicy.name help: What this policy is called when an account or a case is assigned to it.
    %%field SlaPolicy.tier help: The customer tier this policy is written for.
    %%field SlaPolicy.first_response_minutes help: How long a customer may wait to hear from a person, in minutes.
    %%field SlaPolicy.resolution_hours help: How long they may wait for the problem to be fixed, in hours.
    %%field SlaPolicy.business_hours_only help: Set when the clock stops overnight and at weekends.
    %%field SlaPolicy.is_active help: Clear this to retire a policy without disturbing the cases already measured against it.

    %%field Activity.activity_type help: What happened — a call, a meeting, an email, a task.
    %%field Activity.subject help: The activity in one line, as it reads on a timeline.
    %%field Activity.description help: What was said, agreed or found out.
    %%field Activity.status help: Whether this is still to do, in hand, or done.
    %%field Activity.priority help: How urgent it is, for the person whose list it is on.
    %%field Activity.due_at help: When it has to happen by.
    %%field Activity.completed_at help: When it actually did.
    %%field Activity.duration_minutes help: How long it took, for calls and meetings.
    %%field Activity.account_id help: The company this activity was about.
